To create a high-quality testing framework in Cypress that utilizes Docker and captures screenshots, follow these steps:

1. Set up Cypress and Docker

First, ensure you have Cypress and Docker installed on your machine. If you don't have them installed, follow these steps:

- Install Node.js (version 14 or higher) from the official website: https://nodejs.org/en/download/
- Install Cypress using npm: `npm install cypress --save-dev`
- Install Docker Desktop from the official website: https://www.docker.com/get-started/

2. Create a Cypress project

Create a new Cypress project by running the following command:

```
npx cypress create
```

This will create a new Cypress project with default configurations.

3. Set up Docker

Create a Dockerfile in the root directory of your Cypress project with the following content:

```dockerfile
FROM node:14

WORKDIR /app

COPY package*.json ./

RUN npm install

COPY . .

CMD ["npm", "run", "test"]
```

This Dockerfile installs Node.js 14 and copies your Cypress project files into the container. It then runs Cypress tests using the `npm run test` command.

4. Update Cypress configuration

Open the `cypress.json` file and add the following configuration:

```json
{
  "integrationFolder": "cypress/integration",
  "videosFolder": "cypress/videos",
  "screenshotsFolder": "cypress/screenshots",
  "reporter": "mocha-junit-reporter",
  "reporterOptions": {
    "mochaFile": "test-results.xml",
    "preserveFailuresFile": "failures.xml",
    "attachments": [
      {
        "path": "cypress/videos",
        "prefix": "video-",
        "pattern": "*.mp4"
      },
      {
        "path": "cypress/screenshots",
        "prefix": "screenshot-",
        "pattern": "*.png"
      }
    ]
  }
}
```

This configuration sets the folder locations for videos and screenshots, and specifies the `mocha-junit-reporter` as the test reporter. The `attachments` option specifies the paths and prefixes for the videos and screenshots, which will be included in the JUnit XML report.

5. Update package scripts

Open the `package.json` file and add the following scripts:

```json
{
  "scripts": {
    "test": "docker build -t my-cypress-project . && docker run --rm -v $(pwd):/app my-cypress-project",
    "test:headless": "docker build -t my-cypress-project . && docker run --rm -v $(pwd):/app -e Cypress_headless=true my-cypress-project",
    "test:open": "docker build -t my-cypress-project . && docker run --rm -v $(pwd):/app -e Cypress_baseUrl=http://localhost:3000 my-cypress-project",
    "test:e2e": "docker build -t my-cypress-project . && docker run --rm -v $(pwd):/app -e Cypress_baseUrl=http://localhost:3000 my-cypress-project cypress run --spec 'cypress/e2e/**/*'"
  }
}
```

These scripts:

- Build the Docker image and run Cypress tests in a container (`test`)
- Run Cypress tests in headless mode (`test:headless`)
- Run Cypress tests with the browser open (`test:open`)
- Run Cypress end-to-end tests (`test:e2e`)

6. Add screenshot commands

Add screenshot commands to your Cypress tests to capture screenshots when tests fail. You can add them at the beginning or end of your test, or both:

```typescript
describe('My Test', () => {
  it('Should pass', () => {
    cy.visit('/');

    // Capture a screenshot when the test passes
    Cypress.config('viewportWidth', 1280);
    Cypress.config('viewportHeight', 720);
    Cypress.config('pageLoadTimeout', 10000);
    cy.screenshot('passing-test');

    cy.contains('Home');
  });

  it('Should fail', () => {
    cy.visit('/not-found');

    // Capture a screenshot when the test fails
    Cypress.on('fail', (error, runnable) => {
      cy.screenshot({
        error,
        failureReason: runnable.title,
        subject: 'failed-test',
      });
    });

    cy.contains('Not Found');
  });
});
```

This example captures a screenshot when the test passes and a failure screenshot when the test fails.

7. Run tests

To run Cypress tests using Docker, use the following commands:

- Run Cypress tests in a container: `npm run test`
- Run Cypress tests in headless mode: `npm run test:headless`
- Run Cypress tests with the browser open: `npm run test:open`
- Run Cypress end-to-end tests: `npm run test:e2e`

That's it! You now have a high-quality testing framework in Cypress that utilizes Docker and captures screenshots. You can also add other Cypress plugins, such as Cypress Dashboard or Cypress Plugin for Video Recording, to enhance your testing capabilities.

